/**
 * Before-insert discount defaults for OpportunityLineItem.
 *
 * Pricing owns the rules (PricingRules) - this class never decides a discount
 * value itself. It only:
 *   1. works out which lines actually need a default (Discount == null),
 *   2. resolves their product/family in bulk (no SOQL inside the loop), and
 *   3. hands the family to PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(...),
 *      so every rule, including the default branch, stays in PricingRules.
 */
public with sharing class LineItemDiscountHandler {

    /**
     * Sets Discount to the product's standard discount when it is null.
     * A value the rep already entered - including 0 - is never overwritten.
     *
     * @param newLines the Trigger.new list (before insert).
     */
    public static void applyStandardDiscounts(List<OpportunityLineItem> newLines) {
        if (newLines == null || newLines.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // 1. Collect the products we may need a discount for. Only lines whose
        //    Discount is still null are considered, so a rep-entered value (0
        //    included) is never touched and we never query for nothing.
        Set<Id> productIds = new Set<Id>();
        Set<Id> pricebookEntryIds = new Set<Id>();
        Boolean anyLineNeedsDefault = false;

        for (OpportunityLineItem line : newLines) {
            if (line.Discount != null) {
                continue;                     // rep typed a value - keep it, 0 included
            }
            anyLineNeedsDefault = true;
            if (line.Product2Id != null) {
                productIds.add(line.Product2Id);
            } else if (line.PricebookEntryId != null) {
                // CPQ inserts are driven by PricebookEntryId; Product2Id is not
                // always populated yet in a before-insert context.
                pricebookEntryIds.add(line.PricebookEntryId);
            }
        }
        if (!anyLineNeedsDefault) {
            return;                           // every line already carries a discount
        }

        // 2. One query per source - never one query per line. PricingRules.standardDiscountFor(Id)
        //    runs its own SOQL, so calling it inside the loop would cost one query per line and
        //    blow the 100-query limit on a 200-line CPQ quote. The *rules* are still not duplicated:
        //    the values come straight from PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily below.
        Map<Id, String> familyByProductId = new Map<Id, String>();
        if (!productIds.isEmpty()) {
            for (Product2 product : [
                SELECT Family
                FROM Product2
                WHERE Id IN :productIds
            ]) {
                familyByProductId.put(product.Id, product.Family);
            }
        }

        Map<Id, String> familyByPricebookEntryId = new Map<Id, String>();
        if (!pricebookEntryIds.isEmpty()) {
            for (PricebookEntry entry : [
                SELECT Product2.Family
                FROM PricebookEntry
                WHERE Id IN :pricebookEntryIds
            ]) {
                familyByPricebookEntryId.put(entry.Id, entry.Product2.Family);
            }
        }

        // 3. Apply the standard discount, still letting PricingRules decide it.
        //    family may legitimately be null - a product with no Family (misc) or an
        //    unresolvable product - and PricingRules' default branch handles that (= 0).
        for (OpportunityLineItem line : newLines) {
            if (line.Discount != null) {
                continue;
            }

            String family = null;
            if (line.Product2Id != null) {
                family = familyByProductId.get(line.Product2Id);
            } else if (line.PricebookEntryId != null) {
                family = familyByPricebookEntryId.get(line.PricebookEntryId);
            }

            line.Discount = PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(family);
        }
    }
}
```

---

### What I'd change and why

**1. The bug that failed your tests: `if (family == null) continue;`.**
My first version used a single null check both for "this product wasn't found" *and* for "this product has no Family". A product with `Family = null` (your misc / "no family" case) therefore kept `Discount = null` instead of getting the default `0`. That's exactly the two failures: `Misc: expected 0, got null` and `no family: expected 0, got null`. Fixed by removing the guard entirely — I now always call `PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(family)` for every null-discount line, so a null family flows into PricingRules' own default branch and yields `0`. The rules still live only in `PricingRules`; nothing was copied here.

**2. I still do NOT call `PricingRules.standardDiscountFor(line.Product2Id)` inside the loop.**
`standardDiscountFor(Id)` runs its own SOQL query. Per line, a 200-line CPQ quote costs up to 200 queries → `System.LimitException: Too many SOQL queries` (limit 100) and the rep's save rolls back. Instead I do at most two `WHERE Id IN :...` queries and call the existing public `standardDiscountForFamily(String)` overload — same class, same rules, one place. If Pricing would rather I keep a single entry point, the clean request is for them to add a bulk overload (`standardDiscountFor(Set<Id>) → Map<Id, Decimal>`); I'd swap the map in step 2 for that call and change nothing else. I did not touch `PricingRules`.

**3. Resolve the product through `PricebookEntryId` when `Product2Id` is null.**
Before-insert lines from CPQ are keyed on `PricebookEntryId`; `Product2Id` isn't guaranteed to be populated in that context. The fallback costs one extra query only when it's actually needed, and it guarantees the line still gets a default rather than silently staying `null`.

**4. Never let an unresolvable product throw.**
`PricingRules.standardDiscountFor(badId)` would fail with *Query did not return any rows* and roll back the whole load. Reading from a map and falling back to `standardDiscountForFamily(null)` gives the documented default (`0`) instead of an exception.

**5. Null-only check preserves rep-entered `0`.** `if (line.Discount != null) continue;` — a deliberate "no discount" (`0`) is never overwritten.

**6. Thin trigger + testable handler, early exit, no SOQL/DML in loops** — works at the full 200-record trigger batch size and costs zero queries when every line already has a discount.

