/**
 * Weekly renewal reminder service.
 *
 * Called by the scheduled job that collects opportunities whose renewal is due
 * (up to 200 Ids per invocation). For every opportunity the owner receives a
 * plain-text reminder email.
 *
 * Why the send is NOT done one message per Messaging.sendEmail call:
 *   Salesforce allows only 10 Messaging.sendEmail INVOCATIONS per Apex
 *   transaction (Limits.getEmailInvocations() / getLimitEmailInvocations()).
 *   The job passes up to 200 opportunities, so the 11th call throws an
 *   uncatchable System.LimitException ("Too many email invocations: 11"),
 *   aborting the whole transaction and rolling back every email - including
 *   the 10 that would have gone out. That is the exact opposite of the goal.
 *
 *   Calling Messaging.sendEmail(List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage>) ONCE
 *   costs a single invocation for all 200 messages. Per-message isolation is
 *   preserved because the returned SendEmailResult[] is index-aligned with
 *   the message list: a message that is rejected is reported in getErrors()
 *   while every other message in the same call is still delivered, and the
 *   success counter is incremented only for the messages that actually
 *   succeeded.
 *
 * Other notes:
 *   - One bulk SOQL query; nothing is queried inside the loop.
 *   - setTargetObjectId(ownerId) addresses an internal user, so these
 *     reminders do not consume the org's daily allocation for external emails.
 *   - setSaveAsActivity(false) keeps the job from creating one Task per reminder.
 *   - Duplicate / null Ids are de-duplicated so nobody is emailed twice.
 *   - Target-object guard: setTargetObjectId only accepts User/Contact/Lead Ids.
 */
public with sharing class RenewalReminderService {

    /**
     * Sends a renewal reminder to the owner of each supplied opportunity.
     *
     * @param opportunityIds up to 200 Opportunity Ids (nulls and duplicates ignored)
     * @return the number of emails that were sent successfully
     */
    public static Integer sendReminders(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        Integer sentCount = 0;

        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return sentCount;
        }

        // De-duplicate and drop nulls while keeping the job's request cheap.
        Set<Id> idsToProcess = new Set<Id>();
        for (Id opportunityId : opportunityIds) {
            if (opportunityId != null) {
                idsToProcess.add(opportunityId);
            }
        }
        if (idsToProcess.isEmpty()) {
            return sentCount;
        }

        // Single bulk query - never query inside the loop.
        List<Opportunity> opportunities = [
            SELECT Id, Name, CloseDate, OwnerId, Owner.Name
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE Id IN :idsToProcess
            ORDER BY CloseDate ASC
        ];
        if (opportunities.isEmpty()) {
            return sentCount;
        }

        // Build every message first; the loop never touches the governor limits
        // beyond heap. One Messaging.sendEmail invocation covers the whole batch.
        List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage> messages = new List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage>();
        List<Opportunity> messageOpportunities = new List<Opportunity>();
        Integer invocationBudget = Limits.getLimitEmailInvocations() - Limits.getEmailInvocations();
        Integer maxMessagesPerInvocation = 100; // comfortably under the 10-invocation cap for 200 records
        Integer chunkStart = 0;

        for (Opportunity opportunity : opportunities) {
            // Skip records we cannot address (e.g. an owner that is not a User).
            if (opportunity.OwnerId == null
                || opportunity.OwnerId.getSObjectType() != User.SObjectType) {
                continue;
            }

            messages.add(buildMessage(opportunity));
            messageOpportunities.add(opportunity);

            // Flush when the chunk is full, keeping total invocations far below
            // the per-transaction limit of 10 (200 records => 2 invocations).
            if (messages.size() == maxMessagesPerInvocation) {
                sentCount += flush(messages, messageOpportunities, chunkStart);
                chunkStart += messageOpportunities.size();
                messages.clear();
                messageOpportunities.clear();

                // Defensive: if this class were ever called repeatedly inside one
                // transaction, stop cleanly instead of blowing the limit.
                if (Limits.getEmailInvocations() >= invocationBudget) {
                    System.debug(
                        LoggingLevel.WARN,
                        'RenewalReminderService: email invocation budget exhausted; '
                            + (opportunities.size() - chunkStart) + ' reminders skipped.'
                    );
                    return sentCount;
                }
            }
        }

        // Flush any remainder.
        if (!messages.isEmpty()) {
            sentCount += flush(messages, messageOpportunities, chunkStart);
        }

        return sentCount;
    }

    /**
     * Sends one chunk of messages with a single Messaging.sendEmail invocation
     * and counts the results that succeeded.
     *
     * The returned SendEmailResult[] is index-aligned with the message list, so
     * a failure on one message is reported without affecting the others - the
     * original "one bad email must not stop the rest" requirement, satisfied
     * without burning one invocation per record.
     */
    private static Integer flush(
        List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage> messages,
        List<Opportunity> opportunities,
        Integer chunkStart
    ) {
        Integer chunkSent = 0;

        try {
            Messaging.SendEmailResult[] results = Messaging.sendEmail(messages);

            if (results == null || results.isEmpty()) {
                System.debug(
                    LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                    'RenewalReminderService: Messaging.sendEmail returned no results for '
                        + messages.size() + ' message(s).'
                );
                return chunkSent;
            }

            for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
                Messaging.SendEmailResult result = results[i];
                Opportunity opportunity = (i < opportunities.size())
                    ? opportunities[i]
                    : null;
                String label = (opportunity != null)
                    ? String.valueOf(opportunity.Id)
                    : String.valueOf(i);

                if (result != null && result.isSuccess()) {
                    chunkSent++;
                } else {
                    String detail = 'Unknown email error';
                    if (result != null) {
                        Messaging.SendEmailError[] errors = result.getErrors();
                        if (errors != null && !errors.isEmpty()) {
                            detail = errors[0].getMessage();
                        }
                    }
                    System.debug(
                        LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                        'Renewal reminder not delivered for opportunity ' + label + ': ' + detail
                    );
                }
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            // A whole-chunk problem must never abort the scheduled job.
            System.debug(
                LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                'RenewalReminderService: send failed for a chunk of ' + messages.size()
                    + ' message(s): ' + e.getMessage()
            );
        }

        return chunkSent;
    }

    /**
     * Builds a single plain-text reminder for one opportunity's owner.
     */
    private static Messaging.SingleEmailMessage buildMessage(Opportunity opportunity) {
        Messaging.SingleEmailMessage message = new Messaging.SingleEmailMessage();
        message.setTargetObjectId(opportunity.OwnerId);
        message.setSaveAsActivity(false);
        message.setSubject('Renewal due: ' + opportunity.Name);
        message.setPlainTextBody(buildBody(opportunity));
        return message;
    }

    /**
     * Plain-text reminder body that always mentions the close date.
     */
    private static String buildBody(Opportunity opportunity) {
        String closeDateText = (opportunity.CloseDate == null)
            ? 'no close date is set on the opportunity'
            : 'the close date is ' + opportunity.CloseDate.format();

        return 'Hi ' + opportunity.Owner.Name + ',\n\n'
            + 'The renewal for "' + opportunity.Name + '" is due: ' + closeDateText + '.\n\n'
            + 'Please review the opportunity and take the next step before that date.\n\n'
            + 'Thanks,\nSales Operations';
    }
}
```

---

### What was wrong and how it's fixed

1. **The failing check — `Too many Email Invocations: 11`.** A `Messaging.sendEmail(...)` call inside the loop costs **one governor invocation per call**, and Apex allows only **10 per transaction**. With the job handing over 200 Ids, the 11th iteration throws `System.LimitException`, which **cannot be caught**, so the entire scheduled transaction aborts and *all* emails roll back. Your `try/catch` never gets a chance to run. The change that was supposed to protect record #11 from record #10's failure was in fact what killed record #11.

2. **The fix.** Messages are now built in the loop (cheap, no limits) and sent with `Messaging.sendEmail(List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage>)` — **one invocation per chunk of 100**, so a 200-record run costs 2 of the 10 allowed invocations instead of 200. A defensive budget check aborts cleanly if this class were ever invoked repeatedly inside a single transaction.

3. **Your actual requirement — "if one email fails the rest still go out" — is still met, more reliably.** Per-message problems (bad address, disabled user, missing template) are *not* thrown; they come back in `SendEmailResult.getErrors()`, and the returned array is **index-aligned with the input list**, so we can tell exactly which opportunity failed, log it, skip it in the count, and continue. `sendReminders` still returns only the number of emails that succeeded.

4. **Everything else you asked for is unchanged:** `setTargetObjectId(<owner Id>)` (internal user → doesn't draw on the external-email daily allocation), `setSaveAsActivity(false)`, subject `'Renewal due: ' + <name>`, plain-text body mentioning the close date, one bulk SOQL query, and the Id guard so a non-User owner can't blow up the whole batch.

**Other suggestions worth keeping:** add an audit trail (nothing is recorded today since activities are off), consider an Email Template / Org-Wide Address so the subject and body aren't hard-coded in Apex, and make sure your test asserts `Limits.getEmailInvocations()` stays well under 10 for a 200-record run.

